This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \quad0\leq\mu\leq2,
  \\
  \int_{\mathbb{R}^4}| \Delta u|^2-d_\beta^2|u|^2=1.
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{align*}
  The given equation finds critical points of the associated energy functional under the constraint \begin{align*}
  \int_{\mathbb{R}^4}| \Delta u|^2-d_\beta^2|u|^2=1,
  \end{align*}
  where the loss of compactness issue arises due to the critical exponential growth of the nonlinearity. The study leverages minimization in the constraint set, existence of mountain pass, high-order variational method, and Estimations made from Leighton-Wilson inequalities. The equation describes "droplets" and relates to self-trapping
